  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 247 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nModels are gradually coming into better agreement on how the active \nweather pattern will unfold across the region from mid to late week. \nBefore that, in the wake of a cold front, strong high pressure \nbuilding over the Mid-Atlantic will lead to breezy northeast winds \nacross our area on Tuesday and bring in a wedge of cool and cloudy \nair. High temperatures will struggle to climb above 60F within the \nwedge, with highs near 70F outside it in central GA.\n\nBy late Tuesday, the pattern starts to shift as the first of two \nnoteworthy shortwaves slides out of the southern Plains and across \nthe Gulf Coast. Guidance continues to have notable spread on the \ntrack, speed, and strength of this initial wave, but in general has \nstrong south-southwest flow ahead of it sending increased moisture \nup over Georgia by Wednesday morning. Rain chances will gradually \nincrease from south to north throughout the day Wednesday, with \nwidespread rain covering the area by the evening hours. In addition \nto the rain, winds will become quite breezy out of the E/NE as the \npressure gradient strengthens with a low developing off to our \nsoutheast. One of the biggest unknowns with the forecast is how long \nthe moisture and forcing linger over Georgia, which model guidance \nhas relatively poor agreement on. Some have the shortwave quickly \nmoving across the area by Thursday morning, but others have the \nshortwave (and nearby surface low) being stronger and slower, with \nrain lingering through at least Thursday evening. At this time, the \nconsensus from guidance is unsurprisingly right in the middle, with \nrain tapering off by the afternoon, and total rainfall amounts of \naround 0.5-1\" in central GA, and 1-2\" in north GA. A slower and \nstronger system will result in higher rainfall totals and a \npotential flood threat, while a faster system would result in lower \nrainfall totals. Models do come back into better agreement on the \noverall pattern behind this system, with dry and cool air moving in \nfor Friday and Saturday. Stay tuned for forecast updates on the \npotentially impactful system mid-week!",
